# Strata TODO

Sorted easiest first. Tags: [config] = flag or setting only, no code. [ui] = web app only.
[py] = serve/server.py. [eng] = C++ engine. [exists] = already works, just not obvious.
Reorganised and reclassified 2026-10-24, with eight new items added; renumbered the same day
(see NUMBERING below) and three more added: the Enter/polling gap, the slow-death log, and the
turn that ends without usable output.

NUMBERING. Renumbered 2026-10-24. Open items run 54 up, in difficulty order, in one sequence
with no gaps and no repeats. DONE and SHELVED items keep the numbers code comments cite and
live in the final section (9, 11, 12, 34-46). Numbers below 54 are therefore historical: they
appear only in the Done section, or they name work that was done and removed - serve/web/app.js,
serve/server.py, serve/*.py, serve/web/app.css and LESSONS.md cite 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12,
34, 35, 36, 37, 38, 40 and 46, and 3-8 name items no longer in this file at all. That is why open
work was moved ABOVE that band rather than renumbered from 1: reusing those numbers would point
forty code comments at the wrong want. Every "item N" cross-reference in this file was rewritten
against the mapping; there are no dangling ones.

## 1. Trivial - minutes to an hour, one file, no design decision

54.  Auto-scroll stops following when a big block lands (a tool call, a table). [ui] BUG
    The human's diagnosis is correct, and the code shows why. `paint()` at
    serve/web/app.js:1491 does `updateAssistant(el, m, true); scrollDown();` - so
    `nearBottom()` measures the gap AFTER the DOM has already grown. A chunk that adds
    thousands of pixels in one frame leaves the reader more than 120 px from the bottom, so
    the follow switches itself off and never comes back.
    The thinking block already gets this right (app.js:1187 measures `nearEnd(body, 48)`
    before assigning `textContent`), which is why thinking follows and the chat does not.
    Fix: measure before mutating, the same way - `const follow = nearBottom(); ...update...;
    if (follow) s.scrollTop = s.scrollHeight;`. One line moved, plus a test that grows the
    content by more than the slack in one step.
    The slack of 120 px is also worth revisiting: a table row or a code fence is easily
    taller than that.


55.  The sidebar's "Yesterday" is arithmetic, not a calendar day. [ui] BUG
    `groupName` (serve/web/app.js) does `Math.floor((Date.now() - t) / 86400000)`, so a chat
    from 23:00 yesterday read at 01:00 today is "Today", and nothing is ever "Yesterday"
    unless it is 24-48 hours old. The human's report is right.
    Fix: compare calendar dates for Today/Yesterday (local midnight, not UTC - the app shows
    local times elsewhere). "Previous 7 days" and "Previous 30 days" can stay arithmetic if
    preferred, but the same diff makes them calendar-correct for free.


56.  Show the context percent on each message's meta line, with a hover breakdown. [ui]
    Wanted shape: `4,040 tokens · 52.7 tok/s · 5 tool calls (54% context)`, and hovering the
    percentage gives a popup with the parts.
    The data already exists: the stream's final usage chunk carries `prompt_tokens` and
    `completion_tokens` (serve/server.py:4632; app.js:1517 stores it in `usage`), and
    `engine.max_context` is already polled for the gauge. So store `m.usage` on the message
    when the turn ends and add one span to `m.meta`.
    Worth putting in the popup: prompt tokens, how much of the prompt was reused from the
    cache (`reused`, already in the request records), output tokens, and reasoning tokens -
    because "54% full" is much more actionable when you can see that 40 points of it was a
    cached prefix.


57.  A blue dot when a reply finishes in the chat you are on but are not looking at. [ui]
    Item 37 marks a chat you are not in. This is the other half: you are on the right chat,
    scrolled up reading an earlier answer, and the turn finishes below the fold. Today
    nothing tells you.
    Shape: `unread` already exists on the chat record; make it a per-message "not seen"
    derived from whether the reader was at the bottom when the turn ended (the follow
    decision from item 54 is the same measurement - reuse it). Show the dot in the gutter
    slot item 37 added, and clear it when the reader reaches the bottom.
    Careful: do not mark unread just because the reader scrolled up deliberately. That is
    the difference between "you missed this" and "you are reading something else".


58.  Pressing Enter does not poll, so the pill can read Idle for 15 s into a turn. [ui] TRIVIAL
    Measured: neither send() nor setBusy() calls pollNow(). The chain is armed by the previous poll's
    pollDelay (app.js:223 and 232), and with nothing running pollDelay returns POLL_IDLE_MS = 15000
    (app.js:164, 177). So after Enter the next /metrics is whatever was already scheduled, up to 15 s
    away, and the pill still says Idle while the engine reads the prompt. showTab already solves exactly
    this for a tab change (app.js:87). Fix: setBusy(true) calls pollNow(); one scheduler already means it
    cannot fork a second chain (item 40's lesson). Test: drive setBusy(true) in the harness and assert the
    recorded setTimeout delay is 0, and that a second send does not start a second chain.


## 2. Small - a few hours, one or two files, no design decision

59.  A notification sound when a turn finishes and the window is not visible. [ui]
    The want: hear it, the way item 55 ("start_beep") does for the server, but for a finished
    conversation. Gate it on the same condition as the blue dot from item 37 - the turn ended
    in a chat you were not looking at, or the window was hidden.
    Nothing exists today: `grep -c "AudioContext" serve/web/app.js` is 0, and the browser
    blocks autoplay before a first gesture, so it needs the click-armed pattern item 55 hit
    head-on (the rejected client-side attempt for start_beep is in LESSONS.md). A short
    WebAudio oscillator is a few lines; a file asset is not needed.
    Two decisions before building: a sound for "finished while hidden" only, or also for
    "finished in another chat while this window is visible"? And should it be one setting or
    follow the blue dot exactly? Recommend: follow the dot, one setting, off by default.
    Do NOT use the Notification API for the first version - it needs a permission prompt and
    behaves differently on Windows/TDE. Sound first, badge later if wanted.


60.  A three-dot menu on each chat row: Rename, Copy, Export, Delete. [ui]
    Replaces the two hover buttons (`.chat-item__actions`, app.css) with one overflow button
    that opens a menu.
    Cheap parts: Rename and Delete already exist (renameChat, deleteChat) and just move into
    the menu. Copy is a new function but trivial - `navigator.clipboard.writeText(JSON.stringify(chat))`,
    which is also the seed of item 66's machine format.
    The cost is the menu itself: a popover positioned in a 260 px rail that is `overflow-y:
    auto`, so it can be clipped by the sidebar's scroll container. That needs either a
    fixed-position popover or flipping the row's direction near the bottom. Budget more time
    for that than for the actions.
    Watch: the actions already only appear on hover and reserve no width (they are
    `display: none` until hovered, and the state markers live in the left gutter) - keep that
    property, or the titles shrink again.


61.  ETAs while a prompt is being read. [ui] probably, [py] maybe
    Honest assessment: the raw material is there and it is cheap to approximate, but a good
    ETA is not. `live.state === "reading"` with `prompt_read` / `prompt_total` already drives
    the percentage in the pill (app.js renderPill), and prefill rate is measured
    (`prefill_tok_s_mean`). So `(prompt_total - prompt_read) / prefill_rate` is a tooltip away.
    Why it is rough: the read rate is not constant (chunk sizes ramp, the expert cache warms,
    a parked conversation skips the read entirely), and a reused prefix is not read at all,
    so the naive number overshoots on warm chats. Measured on this box: cold reads run
    ~2,000 tok/s and a 533,954-token prompt took 332 s.
    Recommendation: do the tooltip, label it an estimate, and show it only once at least a
    few thousand tokens have been read so the rate is real. Cheap, and the pill already has
    the space. Not worth a server change.


62.  Enable and disable a whole MCP server from the front end. [ui] [py] EASY
    The config already supports "disabled": true per entry, and the server leaves it out with
    one log line. So this is a checkbox that writes that flag - the smallest item in the
    MCP set.
    Care: it writes the run config, so it needs the same origin guard as GET/POST /config,
    and it takes effect only on restart. Say so in the UI.


63.  Fork a conversation (clone, not nested). [ui]
    A chat is an object with a messages array in IndexedDB; a deep copy is short.
    Needs the same care as item 60's Copy: a new id, and `unread`/draft state should not
    carry over.


64.  Add and edit a system prompt, set ONCE when the conversation starts. [ui] [py] EASY
    There is no system-prompt field in the web app today (no system_prompt anywhere in
    app.js), and the server already supports one: a leading system/developer message is
    merged into the template's system turn (chat_template.jinja). So this is a per-chat
    field stored with the chat object in IndexedDB, sent as the first message on every
    request of that chat. Because it never changes mid-chat, it costs nothing: the system
    turn stays identical turn to turn and the pinned root checkpoint keeps matching.
    Editing it LATER is the only case that re-reads the chat once - worth a confirm dialog,
    same as the tool-thinking toggle.
    This is the prerequisite for item 78 (dynamic system prompt) and it makes item 70's
    prefix pin actually useful, so it is worth doing early despite being small.


65.  Trim long tool calls before they reach the DOM. [py] [ui] - decide the policy first
    The question "do we trim?" has a measured answer for this box: `max_result_chars` is
    **500,000** in strata-iq3_s.json (the code default is 20,000), and `max_rounds` is 900.
    So one call can put 500 KB of text into the page, and a long agent turn can do that many
    times. The DOM cap is only visual: `.tool-call__pre` has `max-height: 320px; overflow:
    auto` (app.css:113), which bounds the *box*, not the text node inside it.
    So yes, this can overwhelm the page, and it is partly mitigated already: an open block's
    body is built lazily and only while open (syncToolBody), so a closed call costs nothing.
    The exposure is (a) a call the reader opens, and (b) every result kept in the message
    object and written to IndexedDB.
    Options, in order of preference:
      - cap what goes into the DOM per open block (say 100 KB) with an explicit
        "showing the first 100 KB of 500 KB - Download / Copy full" affordance. Keeps the
        full text for copy and export, bounds the render.
      - keep only a preview in the message object and store the full result once, keyed by
        call id, fetched on demand. Bigger change, but it is the one that fixes IndexedDB
        growth too.
      - lower `max_result_chars` in the config. Cheapest, but it changes what the MODEL
        sees, which is a different decision from what the browser renders - do not conflate
        them.
    Measure before building: how big is a real chat record here, and how many open blocks
    does a 900-round turn actually produce?


66.  Import and export conversations. [ui] [py]
    Export exists but only as human-readable Markdown (the export button). Needs a machine
    format. llama.cpp's chat format is JSONL with a "messages" array - a reader for that is
    small. Import is easy for text; images and tool rounds need care to round-trip.
    Note: tool rounds carry `at` and `rat` offsets that the replay depends on
    (assistantMessages, and serve/server.py:4470-4481 on why they must match). An import
    that loses them re-creates the re-read bug. Round-trip those or refuse the import.


67.  Edit and delete posts in a conversation, mine and the model's. [ui] [py]
    Chat-level rename and delete exist (renameChat, deleteChat); message-level do not.
    Straightforward to build. See item 69 for the cost.
    The delete wants a modal, not a confirm(): "delete this message", a warning that it
    cannot be undone and that the conversation must be re-read (until item 69 lands), and a
    checkbox, **checked by default**, "delete all messages below". Unchecked means delete
    only that one and leave the rest - which is the case that changes the prompt mid-stream
    and therefore the one that re-reads everything after it. The modal should say which of
    the two it is about to do, in token terms if the numbers are available (item 56 already
    computes them).
    Message-level identity is a prerequisite for any of this being safe: messages have no ids
    today, only array position and `time`. See item 76.

NEED RENUMBERING

68.  Queue another conversation while one is going (llama.cpp style). [ui] MEDIUM, not engine work
    The SERVER ALREADY DOES THIS: docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request
    at a time: the others wait in the server's queue." Nothing engine-side is missing, and
    --batch N would run them at once instead of queued.
    What is missing is entirely in the page: one busy flag, one send button disabled
    (app.js:1015), one stream reader. Needs per-request state (controller, target message,
    chat id) so two streams can be tracked and painted into the right chat.

## 3. Medium - two or three layers, or a decision to make first (days)

69.  Make editing or deleting not re-read everything. [eng] [py]
    Partly possible today: STRATA_CACHE_MESSAGE_BOUNDARY=1 saves a checkpoint at the previous
    turn boundary so a replaced final message reuses the history before it. Limits from
    docs/MESSAGE_BOUNDARY_CACHE.md: prefixes of at least 8,192 tokens, a tail of at most
    1,024, off for layer splits, opt-in, and it can split prefill chunks. Editing near the
    end is cheap; editing early in a long chat is not, because every later token changes.
    Real feasibility: good for the last few messages, no help in the middle.

70.  Pin one conversation so it is always fast to open: in RAM while Strata runs, on disk
    when it does not. [py] [eng] [ui] HARD - and the one item here that needs a decision
    before code. This absorbs three earlier items that turned out to be the same want
    (keep the last conversation on disk / pin one so it is never evicted / always fast to
    open), because they are not three features.
    THE WANT, from the human: a button on a chat saying "this one stays cached". Open it
    later - even after Strata was stopped and started again - and it resumes fast instead
    of re-reading. NOT already done: what exists is a per-SLOT save/restore of whatever is
    loaded at that moment, which is not a per-conversation promise and does not survive a
    server restart.
    THREE MECHANISMS THAT ALREADY EXIST, and what each is missing:
      - the live conversation cache (park/restore in RAM). Works; measured 326 ms for
        193,453 tokens. But it is LRU with a byte budget, so it makes no "always" promise -
        a 6.7K side chat evicted a 195K conversation on this box and cost a 107 s re-read.
      - the checkpoint pin (--prompt-cache-root, and "strata_prefix" on a request). Pins a
        PREFIX, never a conversation, and exactly one at a time (generate.cpp:11169 refuses
        a pin below --prompt-cache 3).
      - session files (POST /slots/0?action=save|restore). Real, validated, per-slot, manual.
        Not keyed to a conversation and never automatic.
    THE NUMBERS THAT FRAME IT (measured here 2026-10-23):
      - parked state: 19.1 KB per token (193,453 tokens = 3.55 GiB)
      - re-read: ~2,000 tok/s, so ~97 s for that chat; a 533,954-token prompt took 332 s
      - NVMe (FireCuda 520, ext4, oflag=direct, 3 GiB): 2.3 GB/s write, 3.2 GB/s cold read
      - a 1M-token conversation: ~18 GiB of state -> ~8 s to write, ~6 s to read, against
        ~500 s to re-read. RAM cannot hold one at that size (budget 4096 MiB; 34 GiB
        available with 35 GiB already in swap), so at 1M "always cached" MUST mean disk.
    WHAT IS MISSING, in order:
      1. Conversation identity. Reuse is exact token-prefix matching with no client session
         ID (DETAILS.md, "Multiple conversations"), so there is nothing to hang a flag on.
         The web app has a chat id in IndexedDB; the server has never seen it. Either send
         it on the request or key on a hash of the conversation's first tokens.
      2. A per-conversation file store: one file per chat id, written on idle/unload, read
         on open, with retention. The session-file format already does the hard part
         (STRSESS header, validated against model and config fingerprint, checkpoints + K/V
         + draft K/V) and the RAM-headroom guard is already applied to save and restore
         (--conversation-cache-min-free-mib 2560).
      3. The switch itself. 18 GiB is ~6 s of I/O on the single FIFO slot, so opening a
         pinned chat while another generates needs a queue or a second engine (see the
         parallel and replicas items).
    SPECIFIED: docs/CONVERSATION_PINNING.md (2026-10-23) - what exists, the measured
    cost per token (19,560 bytes from the park log), the four phases, the limits that
    will bite, and the decision being asked for. Start there, not here.
    Read it before writing code. Phase 0 in that spec is a measurement with the
    existing manual save/restore, and it answers the question everything else depends
    on: does a restored conversation actually get matched by the client's next prompt.

    DO NOT START WITH CODE. Answer the design question first: for chats near 1M tokens is
    "always cached" a disk promise (~6 s to resume, ~18 GiB of free disk per chat) or a RAM
    promise (impossible at that size here)? That answer drives all three steps, and it
    belongs in docs/ before it belongs in serve/.


71.  Close the browser and keep the conversation running. [py] [ui]
    Today a disconnect ends the request: GeneratorExit at server.py:4171 settles it, and the
    docs advertise that closing the connection really stops the model. Needs generation
    detached from the HTTP response, a server-side buffer of what it produced, and a way to
    re-attach. Also interacts with the idle-unload and watchdog paths. This is the biggest
    item in this tier.


72.  reasoning_loop_recovery "recover" fires and then the turn freezes at the same token count. [py] [eng] MEDIUM
    Written up in strata-slow-death.txt at the repo root (untracked; it is full of literal control tags, so
    read it but never quote from it - that is how two turns died today). The shape, twice in one run:
    "repeated reasoning detected at 2560 tokens (coverage=0.555); resuming the same output with the
    low-effort instruction (reasoning_loop_recovery)", after which the thinking counter sits at exactly 2560
    for minutes while the reported rate decays 88.5 -> 77.8 -> ... -> 29.3 tok/s. The turn neither advances
    nor ends, and the client eventually gives up. This is the SLOW death: a different death from the
    forged-boundary one (item 83) and from the no-op one (item 73).
    It DOES fire on this box: the web app sends reasoning_effort "high", the template maps high to xhigh
    (serve/chat_template.jinja:59-61) and xhigh is the default, so the sentence the splice looks for is
    present - which is why the log shows the rescue running and then the freeze following it.
    WHY XHIGH SPECIFICALLY, and why not always: focused_recovery_prompt (serve/server.py:193) splices token
    ids. It finds the whole-token subsequence HIGH_EFFORT (server.py:157, = EFFORT_TEXT["xhigh"]) inside the
    first system message, swaps it for LOW_EFFORT (server.py:158), and appends every id generated so far. It
    splices ids rather than re-rendering because the prompt is never decoded and encoded again, so a literal
    tag or vision marker inside it stays what it was (#537, #554). It looks for xhigh because that is the
    template's default sentence and the point of the recovery is to LOWER effort mid-turn; it has no idea
    what level the request asked for. When the request said low or medium the xhigh sequence is not there and
    it returns None - that is item 73, not this one. So "why not always" means: key the splice on the
    request's own level. EFFORT_TEXT is already keyed by level (server.py:134), so that change is small.
    TWO MEASUREMENTS OWED before fixing the freeze: why the counter stops advancing at all, and why the
    reported rate halves while it does not. The decay alone hints at re-decoding the same window; that is a
    guess and needs the engine side of the exchange to confirm. No engine rebuild was attempted here.
73.  "recover" is a silent no-op for a request that asks low or medium effort. [py] TRIVIAL fix, one decision
    focused_recovery_prompt (serve/server.py:193) looks only for the xhigh sentence. A request that asked low
    or medium carries that level's sentence instead, so the token subsequence is absent, the function returns
    None, and the loop is detected with "recover" configured while nothing is recovered. Measured in
    strata-slow-death.txt: a 63-token prompt at effort_position "start" has no xhigh sentence and returns
    None; at "end" the sentence is present and it returns 93 ids.
    Who is bitten: not this box's own web traffic (the app sends high, mapped to xhigh at
    chat_template.jinja:59-61), but any other client that asks low or medium - common as soon as a config or
    an app sets reasoning_effort low. This config sets no effort_position, so it is the default "start".
    Fix: pass the request's level into focused_recovery_prompt() and splice THAT sentence down to low
    (EFFORT_TEXT is keyed by level already, server.py:134). Decide first what "down to low" means when the
    request is already low: skip the recovery, or use a different nudge. Add a test that renders a low-effort
    prompt and asserts the recovery is not None - that is the case silently missing today. And the rescue's
    log line should not claim a recovery it did not perform.

## 4. Large - engine work, or a new subsystem (weeks)

74.  Run two conversations at the SAME TIME, not queued. [eng] [py] [ui]
    Distinct from 10 (which is queuing, already supported). True simultaneity is
    "parallel": N (--batch N), docs/BATCHING.md, measured +31-39% total throughput with
    2-4 clients on one GPU. Two hard caveats for this box:
      - The engine keeps no pipelined slot as a conversation cache (server.py:1656), so
        --batch fights the parking that fixes the re-read problem. Pick one behaviour or
        design the interaction.
      - Slots share the engine context: 4 chats x 200K tokens = 800K of a 1M context, and
        each held or parked conversation costs RAM (measured here: ~19 KB per token).
    Feasible, but it is a capacity decision, not a feature flag.

75.  Preload conversations when I start typing. [py] [eng]
    Restoring a parked or on-disk conversation before the request lands needs conversation
    identity (see 16) and a restore path that is not tied to a slot. Also speculative
    loading competes with the request that is actually coming. Worth revisiting after 16
    and 17 exist.

76.  Back the conversation cache with NVMe instead of RAM. [eng]
    Your drive measured 2.3 GB/s write, 3.2 GB/s cold read (FireCuda 520, ext4, oflag=direct,
    3 GiB). A 3.5 GiB snapshot of this conversation is ~1.5 s to write and ~1.1 s to read,
    against ~100 s to re-read 195K tokens - roughly 90x faster than the current fallback, and
    50 GB free holds about 14 such conversations versus 1 in 4 GiB of RAM.
    Strata already does this for experts (the file tier, --mmap-experts, --resident-budget-gib)
    so the plumbing pattern exists; a file tier for the park cache is new engine code plus
    eviction policy. Note the docs warn a parked conversation being paged is itself a stall
    cause (#2049, #2065), so this needs the same care the expert tier got.

77.  Dynamic elements in the system prompt (date, system info) - frozen at chat start. [ui] [py]
    With 18 done, this is a one-time fill: compute the date and system info when a chat is
    created, store them in the chat's system prompt, and never recompute them. Then the
    system turn is byte-identical for the whole chat and the pinned root keeps matching.
    Do NOT recompute per request: anything that changes in the front of the prompt breaks
    the shared prefix for the entire chat. The server already fights exactly this for
    Claude Code, which stamps a per-request billing header into its system prompt; it pins
    those stamps so the prompt stays identical turn to turn (DETAILS.md). If a value really
    must change mid-chat, "effort_position": "end" is the existing pattern to copy: put the
    varying text in a short turn right before the answer, after the checkpoint.


78.  Warm the conversation while I type, and cancel the warming when I switch.
    [py] [eng] [ui] HARD, and the description here replaced a wrong one (it used to say
    "cancel when switching", which is a different, much smaller feature).

    WHAT IS WANTED
      - Open a chat whose state is parked in RAM or saved on disk -> bring it back into RAM
        the moment it gets focus / I start typing, not when I press Enter.
      - Start a new chat in a conversation that was cold -> begin reading the shared prefix
        while I type, so the 300-second part is already finished by the time I send.
      - Switch to a different chat mid-typing -> CANCEL that speculative read, so it does
        not compete with the request I actually send.
    The measured cost it removes: a cold 533,954-token prompt took 332 s in the log today.
    Starting that at focus instead of at Enter is the whole point.

    WHY IT IS HARD - three blockers, in order of how much they hurt
      1. There is no way to say "prepare X" without sending a prompt. The only engine verbs
         are GEN (read a prompt AND generate) and the session save/restore pair. A warm-up
         would either generate tokens nobody asked for, or need a new engine command
         ("read to here, hold, generate nothing"). That new verb is the real work.
      2. The engine serves ONE request at a time (docs/BATCHING.md: "By default Strata serves
         one request at a time: the others wait in the server's queue"). A speculative read
         of an old chat therefore BLOCKS the real request if you press Enter while it runs -
         which is worse than today. So cancel-on-switch is not a refinement; it is a
         precondition. And cancellation mid-read is already delicate: a cancelled read
         checkpoints what it got to and the retry resumes from there (seen today: 57,344 read
         then resumed at 55,758), so the partial work is not wasted - that helps here.
      3. "The conversation" has no identity. Reuse is exact token-prefix matching with no
         client session ID (DETAILS.md, "Multiple conversations"). So the server cannot be
         told "warm chat 7"; it can only be handed a token prefix. The client would have to
         render the whole history to know what to warm - which is the expensive part it is
         trying to avoid, and is the same reconstruction that caused the re-read bug today.

    WHAT ALREADY EXISTS - and it covers maybe half of this
      - Parked conversations restore in ~0.3 s per ~190K tokens (measured today: 326 ms for
        193,453 tokens, 3.55 GiB) versus ~330 s to re-read. So for a PARKED chat, warming is
        already fast; the gap is that parking happens on switch-AWAY, not on focus.
      - --conversation-cache-min-tokens decides which chats get a slot at all (see 16/17).
      - Session files (save/restore) already put a conversation on disk - item 70 automates
        that, and 16 is therefore a prerequisite for the "stored on hard drive" half.
      - The pinned system-prompt root (6,606 tokens here) means a new chat in the same app
        already reuses the common prefix without any warming.

    SMALLEST USEFUL STEP, before any of the hard parts
      A client-side prefetch that costs no engine work: on focus/first keystroke, render the
      history and POST it with max_tokens=1 and "strata_checkpoint": false. That reads the
      prompt, checkpoints it, and generates one token - so the real send reuses the prefix.
      Caveats that must be handled: it still occupies the single FIFO slot (so cancel must
      work, and aborting mid-read is the same path as today), it spends the 332 s whether or
      not you send, and it pollutes /metrics and the counters with a request you never
      meant. Gate it behind a setting and label it in the status line.
      Order I would build: 17 (auto save/restore) -> 18 (pin one chat) -> this prefetch ->
      then the engine-side "read, hold, generate nothing" verb if the prefetch is not enough.


## 5. Added 2026-10-23: MCP servers from the front end

79.  Add and remove MCP servers in the web app. [ui] [py] MEDIUM
    Reading is done: GET /mcp returns every server, its state and its tools, and the Monitor
    lists them (app.js:402-432, the mcp-card). What is missing is writing.
    The plumbing exists: the Settings view already edits the run config (#564, svc.config_path,
    GET/POST /settings), and the config's "mcp_servers" block is plain JSON in the same shape
    as Claude Desktop's mcpServers. So this is a form on top of the config write that already
    happens, not a new subsystem.
    Care: a server entry is a COMMAND LINE that runs on this PC with your rights (SECURITY.md,
    docs/MCP_SERVER.md "Safety"). Adding one from a browser page needs the same origin check
    the MCP tools already use (_own_page), and an API key when the server is reachable from
    other devices. Starting/stopping a server also restarts the engine's tool list, which
    changes the system turn and costs every chat one re-read (see 18's note).

80.  Enable and disable INDIVIDUAL tools. [py] [ui] MEDIUM
    Nothing today: McpHub offers every tool of an enabled server as <server>__<tool> and
    routes a call back to whichever server has it (serve/mcp.py). Needs a per-tool exclusion
    list in the config, filtered in McpHub.template_tools(exclude=...) - that parameter is
    already there and already used to drop the request's own tools on a name clash, so the
    hook exists. Also worth showing the filtered count in the mcp-card so it is obvious which
    tools the model can actually see.



## 6. Bug: the turn that delivers nothing, and the leaking tags

81.  Leaked markup in the answer: literal backslash-n pairs, closing think tags, closing
    thinking tags. [py] [ui] - this is the one you are seeing in the window
    All three symptoms are one event: text that LOOKS like structure being READ as
    structure, by whichever layer is consuming the stream. Three layers have done it to me
    today - Strata's OutputParser, the MCP transport, and the harness that renders my own
    reasoning. Each assumes the model's structure arrives as plain text and must be
    re-recognised by the reader, so every reader is a place it can go wrong.
    Part of it is innocent: the vocabulary really does contain backslash-n as a single token
    (ByteLevel BPE), so a model that writes those two characters in prose gets them back.
    The tags are the harmful case.
    Two real defects found in Strata's own parser, both now gated:
      - a closing think tag quoted on its own line inside a code fence is treated as the
        real end, so the thinking stops mid-example and the rest of the fence plus the real
        answer becomes the answer. #537 only catches the tag right after a quote or a
        backtick. Fixed behind "fence_quoted_thinking" (91927798, OFF by default).
      - a tool call the model opens and never finishes is dropped silently: the answer ends
        where the call began and no call runs. Now counted and logged (a69b91c3, 75f1774a).
    WHY A BETTER PARSER MAY BE IMPOSSIBLE: the shipped corpus
    (serve/fixtures/rcall_specimens.json, 37 recorded real cases) rejected a rule that
    looked obviously right - "a real end is always followed by a line break". One real
    specimen has the tag followed directly by text, and the correct answer is that the text
    IS the answer. Quoted and genuine tags arrive byte-identical, so no local rule can tell
    them apart. This codebase has already produced both directions of the bug (#804, then
    #1058 fixing an over-correction of #804).
    THE STRUCTURAL FIX: stop representing boundaries as text. The engine already knows them
    as token IDs - 248068/248069 the think pair, 248058/248059 the tool_call pair,
    248045/248046 the turn pair, all marked special in token_type.json - and it sends raw
    IDs on its T lines today; the server detokenises them and throws the ID away one line
    later (serve/server.py:3995). Roughly 80-120 lines, two files, IF the engine forwards
    those IDs on T lines (UNVERIFIED - check generate.cpp:11818; if it filters them, add a
    small C++ change plus a CUDA/HIP/SYCL rebuild per AGENTS.md).

82.  The model stopping before or while writing tool calls.
    Same failure as 28. Measured on this box over 200 rounds: 5 turns ended giving the
    client neither answer text nor a completed call, and 13 turns were rescued by
    reasoning_close_retry. The engine finished everything and the parser dropped nothing,
    so it was never the engine.
    INSTRUMENTED (75f1774a): the server now prints once per bad turn -
        [strata] EMPTY TURN: the reply delivered no answer and no completed call
                 (state=rcall held='...' abandoned=1 reasoning_tokens=142 rescued=0 refused=0)
    and /metrics counts totals.empty_turns. It observes only; it rescues nothing.
    PROVEN NOT TO BE THE FIX: the rescue behind "reasoning_close_retry": "calls" (778cfab5)
    targets parser state "rcall" - a call opened INSIDE the reasoning. That state has never
    appeared in the real log. Every observed death showed a "writing a tool call" phase, and
    tool_start is emitted from exactly one method (_scan, frontend.py:1023), which only runs
    from state "call" - the answer, after the thinking closed. Different state, different
    shape. The test that "verified" the widening (ReasoningCloseRetryHeldCall) used a
    synthetic rcall input I made up; it proves the mechanism works for a shape nobody has
    observed. So: the widening is not a fix, and the rungs of this ladder were built in the
    wrong order.
    WHAT THE OBSERVED SHAPE DOES: with stream_tools on (the server always sets it), a call
    announced but not completed is DROPPED at finish() - frontend.py:1378 clears the buffer
    and returns, so the client gets neither answer nor call. That is the empty turn. The
    leaked-markup path (finish() printing CALL_START + the held body as content,
    frontend.py:1399) is a different branch, reached when the call was never announced.
    Both are counted by abandoned_tool_calls; only the first is counted by empty_turns.
    Which branch the real deaths took is still NOT directly measured - the probe was not
    running for them. It is now, so the next one will say.
    Do not assume the leaked tags in the window came from either branch: several were
    provably my own text, written while discussing the tags.
    STILL TO MEASURE: totals.empty_turns counts the genuinely-empty turns; the leaked-markup
    turns are NOT counted by it today. Add that (a call abandoned in state call/json is
    already counted in abandoned_tool_calls) and correlate it with what the window shows.


83.  Nothing notices a turn that ends without usable output, and nothing continues it. [py] [ui] MEDIUM
    Asked as "you should never stop without a final output - surely the backend or the front end could see
    that and continue you". All three recovery flags are already on here (strata-iq3_s.json:
    reasoning_close_retry true, reasoning_loop_recovery "recover", tool_call_recovery true) and sessions
    still die.

    Measured over 2,024 requests in /tmp/strata-server.log:
      - 10 ended with 0 tokens, every one finish=cancel and every one mid "reading the prompt" (46 s,
        90 s, 40 s, 24 s). The client giving up on a long prefill, not a stall.
      - 16 ended finish=stop with <= 80 tokens; the most common phase set among them was
        {writing a tool call} alone (6 of 16) - no thinking phase, no answering phase.
      - 19 logged "the reply ended inside its thinking with no answer" (reasoning_close_retry rescued
        them), 5 logged "tool calls inside the thinking", and 0 logged EMPTY TURN. The counter built for
        this cannot see the failure, and a counter that cannot see the bug is worse than none (LESSONS.md).
      - The recovery flags are not the answer to this one. "recover" fires on this box and then the turn
        freezes at the same token count (item 72); it does nothing at all for a request that asked low or
        medium (item 73); and neither shape is what killed today's turns.

    WHAT KILLED TWO TURNS TODAY, observed live: the agent quoted a line of raw model output while
    investigating this bug, that text contained a literal closing thinking tag, a consumer read it as a
    real boundary, and the turn ended mid-sentence with no final output. Same failure as item 81 -
    boundaries represented as text can be forged by the content - reached from the other side: not the
    model forging one, the agent quoting one. It raised no EMPTY TURN line, because it is not an empty
    turn: it is a turn that ends early with plausible text.

    THREE THINGS THAT COULD NOTICE IT, cheapest first:
      1. The client that runs the agent: detect "turn ended with no final text and no tool call" and
         re-prompt. Needs no Strata change, and it is the only one that covers the quoted-tag death, where
         the truncation is between the model and its consumer rather than in the parser.
         THE FRONT END CAN ALREADY TELL. app.js:1342 computes exactly this predicate -
         `hasOutput = !!m.text || !!(m.tools && m.tools.length) || !!m.error` - and item 34 uses it only to
         hide the bubble and the meta row. Nothing acts on it. So the web app could re-prompt, or at least
         offer a Continue control, from that one boolean. Two things to decide before building: the web app
         is a chat UI, so an automatic re-prompt is a policy change and should be opt-in (it spends a real
         turn and can loop), and it only covers the web app - the agent harness is a separate client, so the
         same check has to exist there too before it would have prevented the deaths in this session. The
         smaller, safer half is a Continue affordance the reader presses, not an automatic loop.
      2. Strata's own rescue, which covers one shape only (reasoning_close_retry, 19 rescues here). It does
         not cover "the answer ended with a tool call that was never completed" - that is item 82's empty
         turn, counted by totals.empty_turns, which reported 0 in this log.
      3. The structural fix, item 81: stop representing boundaries as text. The engine has them as token
         IDs and the server discards the ID at server.py:3995. Only that makes both directions impossible.
    NOT ESTABLISHED: whether the engine forwards the special IDs on its T lines - check generate.cpp:11818
    before sizing item 81. And any "did this turn end properly" check has to tell "short but complete" from
    "stopped early", which length alone cannot: these deaths ended with a plausible sentence.

## 7. Added 2026-10-23: server-side conversation log, and offline queueing

84.  The server writes the conversation to disk (flag on, configurable dir, optional gzip).
    [py] MEDIUM - and it is the instrument the glitch in section 6 actually needs
    Today the server keeps only in-memory numbers: Service.history is a deque(maxlen=500) of
    per-request stats, read by GET /metrics?requests=all. Nothing about the actual messages
    is kept, so when a turn delivers nothing there is no record of what the model wrote.
    That is why this took so long to pin down - the evidence left no trace.
    A hook already exists, and it is closer to done than this item assumed: with
    STRATA_DEBUG=1 the server prints a "req" line per request AND, at server.py:4253, the
    model's ENTIRE raw generated text ("raw: ...") for every request. That is the ground
    truth the whole diagnosis was missing, and it needs no code - it is one env var, now set
    in strata-iq3_s.json. _debug_req (server.py:4373) keeps only an 80-char preview of the
    last message, which cannot show a stray tag, but the raw line can.
    What is still missing, and what this item is actually about: durability and shape. The
    raw dump goes to stdout (so it lives only as long as the tee file), it is not keyed to a
    conversation, there is no retention, and it is unbounded - with max_rounds 900 on a
    700K-token chat it will grow fast.
    Build: "conversation_log_dir" plus "conversation_log_gzip": true. Append-only JSONL, one
    record per turn: the messages as received, the rendered prompt's tail, the model's raw
    output, and the parser's verdict - delivered / finished_in / held_at_end / abandoned /
    rescued / refused, all of which now exist on OutputParser. gzip is stdlib (serve/ uses no
    gzip or zlib today, so it is new but trivial).
    Care: this stores full conversation text on disk. It needs the same origin and host
    guards as the other admin endpoints, a stated retention policy, and it must not write
    request bodies that carry an API key.

85.  Queue a request when the server is down and send it when it is back. [ui] MEDIUM
    Today the page just errors. The SERVER side is already right: requests queue behind the
    FIFO (docs/BATCHING.md - "By default Strata serves one request at a time: the others
    wait in the server's queue"), and a restart never hangs a waiter - it ends with a clean
    503 (serve/test_restart_waiters.py, #1012). So this is entirely client-side.
    Needs: an outbox in IndexedDB (the store already exists), a health poll, ordering rules,
    and a decision about a second message arriving while one is queued. It must survive a
    reload, which is what makes it an IndexedDB problem rather than an array.

86.  Related: item 71 (close the browser, keep the conversation running) is the server side of
    the same wish. With 31 in place, 21 gets much easier - the output is already on disk, so
    a reconnect can read it instead of needing a live stream.

87.  Multiple tabs open at once, kept in sync. [ui] MEDIUM - and it splits into two problems
    of very different size.
    Today: nothing. app.js has no BroadcastChannel and no "storage" event listener, so two
    tabs are two independent apps over one IndexedDB. Worse, every save writes the WHOLE chat
    (saveChat / store.set("chat", ...)), so the tab that saves last silently overwrites the
    other's work. Two tabs open now is already a data-loss bug, not just a missing feature.

    EASY HALF - chat-list and metadata sync. [ui] roughly a day
    A BroadcastChannel("strata") plus three messages: chat created, renamed, deleted, and
    "chat N changed". Handlers refresh the sidebar and, if the open chat is the one that
    changed, reload its messages. Rename and delete are whole-record operations, so they
    broadcast cleanly.
    Needs two things the store does not have:
      - a revision or updatedAt per chat, so a save can be rejected when the record changed
        under it (compare-and-put), otherwise the sync makes the clobbering more visible,
        not less;
      - tombstones for deletes, so a tab that still holds a deleted chat does not write it
        back on its next save.
    Deleting a chat while the OTHER tab is streaming into it needs a decision: stop that
    stream, or let it finish and drop the result.

    HARD HALF - one answer visible in both tabs, and queueing from both. [py] [ui]
    The server side is already right: requests queue behind the FIFO (docs/BATCHING.md -
    "the others wait in the server's queue"), so two tabs sending at once is safe and the
    second simply waits. Nothing to build there.
    What cannot be done client-side is sharing the STREAM. An in-flight answer belongs to one
    tab's fetch and AbortController; the other tab cannot attach to it, and if the owning tab
    closes, the generator's GeneratorExit (serve/server.py:4171) ends the request. So a
    second tab shows nothing until a reload.
    That is item 71 wearing a different hat: the generation has to be detached from the
    connection that started it, buffered server-side, and re-attachable. Do 21 (and 31, which
    puts the output on disk) first and this becomes a poll - much smaller than building it
    now.
    Order I would take: fix the clobbering (revision + tombstones) -> BroadcastChannel for
    the list -> 21/31 -> shared stream.


