39.  Static assets: a validator, so a revalidation is a 304 and not the whole file. [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    `Cache-Control: no-cache` on /web/* with NO ETag or Last-Modified meant every revalidation was a full
    200 - 29,112 bytes for the icon sprite, which the page re-resolves as output streams. The page itself
    carried no Cache-Control at all, which is why a changed app.js sometimes needed a hard-refresh.
    Shipped: `_send_static` in serve/server.py serves /web/* and both pages with ETag + Last-Modified,
    honours If-None-Match (including `*` and a list of tags) and If-Modified-Since, and answers 304 with no
    body. The bytes are held in memory against the file's mtime and size, so an edit is picked up on the next
    request with no restart and the disk is not read once per asset per tab per second. no-cache is kept on
    purpose: a developer editing app.js must see it immediately.
    Tests: serve/test_static_cache.py (11), added to the fast suite list in HANDOFF.md.


40.  Metrics: ask for less, and less often. [py] [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Measured before the change: /metrics is 7,317 bytes per call, polled once a second unconditionally -
    poll() rescheduled whatever tab was showing and whether the window was visible. About 26 MB an hour per
    open tab. Of that payload `history` alone is 4,636 bytes and engine / requests / hardware /
    conversation_cache are Monitor-only; the header pill reads about 256 bytes, 3.5% of it.
    Shipped, both halves:
      - the server answers `GET /metrics?lite=1` with {live, engine, time, lite}. Not only fewer bytes: the full
        path snapshots the telemetry AND calls conv_log.poll, which reads up to a MiB of new engine-log lines
        (READ_MAX) - the Chat tab was paying for that once a second and had nothing to show for it. A test
        counts those calls and asserts a lite answer makes neither.
      - the page asks for lite off the Monitor and About tabs, and the cadence follows what is happening:
        1 s while a request of ours is in flight or the state is reading/generating/running-tool/queued,
        15 s idle, 60 s when the window is hidden (document.hidden); the Monitor keeps its own 1 s because it
        has sparklines to feed. A tab switch or the window coming back polls at once.
      - one scheduler (schedulePoll clears the pending handle) so pollNow() means "now" and cannot fork a
        second chain; startup no longer polls twice, since showTab already schedules the first.
    The pill logic moved into renderPill(), which both the full and the lite path call, so the two cannot
    drift. A lite answer is merged into the last full one, so switching to the Monitor paints immediately
    instead of waiting a tick.
    Tests: serve/test_static_cache.py's LiteMetrics (payload keys, size, a running tool still named in the
    lite answer, and the telemetry/conv_log call counts) and serve/test_chat_sidebar.js's case 42 (the URL per
    tab, and every cadence). Controls: asking full from the Chat tab, ignoring document.hidden, and making the
    server do the work anyway each fail.
    NOT done here, deliberately: the ?history=0 knob (lite supersedes it for the Chat tab) and any change to
    what the Monitor itself asks for - it needs all of it. Item 41 (SSE) would replace the polling entirely.
    Followed up the same day, because the human asked "why does About get full metrics?" and I had never checked:
    About was asking for the whole 9,686-byte answer to draw 1,282 bytes of it (engine + hardware +
    hardware_static = 13%). Now three tiers, and the split is about the WORK, not just the bytes:
      ?lite=1            {live, engine}                    no telemetry snapshot, no engine-log read
      ?lite=1&hw=1       + hardware, hardware_static      telemetry snapshot only, no engine-log read
      (none)             everything                        telemetry AND conv_log.poll (up to a MiB of new
                                                          engine-log lines), which only conversation_cache needs
    About also backs off to 15 s idle now - its facts change rarely. renderLite() paints the pill and, when the
    answer carries them, About's cards; without that call the lite+hw keys would arrive and be dropped.
    Lesson worth keeping: I had asserted "About draws cards, so it needs the full answer" without reading which
    keys renderAbout() touches. It touches three.


41.  SSE /events: push state transitions instead of polling for them. [py] [ui] SHELVED 2026-10-24 - the
    human's call, and the numbers support it: after items 39-42 the Chat tab asks ~404 bytes every 15 s when
    idle and nothing at all when the window is hidden, so the case for a pushed connection is much weaker than
    it looked. Revisit only if the Monitor tab's own 1 s full poll becomes a problem, or if item 68 (several
    streams) makes state changes too frequent to poll for.
    One connection per tab pushing small frames on a state change and when a turn finishes - which is also
    what makes item 37 (an unread reply in another conversation) and item 68 (queued conversations) solid
    rather than polling-driven. The cost to accept: serve() is a ThreadingHTTPServer, so a stream that never
    ends parks a thread for the life of the tab, and it interacts with the idle-unload timer and the watchdog,
    both of which reason about live connections. protocol_version is HTTP/1.0 (SSE ends by closing), so the
    client needs a reconnect loop.
    PRECONDITION, and this should not ship without it: the chat write is a blind whole-record put (writeChat
    -> objectStore.put, no read, no revision) and the in-memory list is a snapshot taken once by allChats() in
    openHistory, with no BroadcastChannel and no storage listener. Two tabs on one chat already lose data
    (item 87): tab A finishes a turn and writes three messages, tab B - still holding its old copy - writes
    one, and A's turn is gone from disk while still on A's screen. Eight call sites do this, and push
    multiplies the writers. The minimum is a compare-and-put inside the existing IndexedDB transaction (read
    the record's revision in the same readwrite transaction, refuse the write if it moved, re-read) - about
    40-60 lines, one file, no server change, testable against the stub IndexedDB the harness already has.
    Message ids and merge-on-conflict stay with item 87; server-side conversation identity stays with item
    16, whose design decision is still unmade. Anything that touches the prompt path is out of scope: a
    checkpoint mismatch costs a re-read of minutes, and that incident already happened once.



42.  The icon sprite is inlined into the pages that use it. [py] [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    The validator (item 39) made each sprite revalidation cheap, but the revalidation was still happening: a
    browser resolves <use href="web/sprite.svg#i-name"> by fetching that file, and it re-resolves it as new
    icons are created while output streams. Same-document references never touch the network at all.
    Shipped: serve/server.py's _page_bytes inserts the sprite's symbols right after the opening body tag of any
    page that contains <use href="#i-, and index.html's 13 references and app.js's SPRITE constant now use
    "#i-name". Only the 21,269 bytes of symbols are inlined - the file also carries a 7,843-byte C2PA provenance
    manifest, which is about the file and has no business in every page; the file on disk keeps its manifest and
    the /web/sprite.svg route still serves it unchanged. monitor.html references no symbols today, so it is left
    exactly as it is.
    The page's ETag now covers the page AND the sprite (both mtimes and sizes), so editing the sprite changes the
    page's validator and the next reload picks it up with no restart.
    Tests: serve/test_static_cache.py's SpriteInlined class - symbols present and before the first use, no
    reference to the file, no metadata in the page, exactly 33 symbols, the page still a sane size, a
    symbol-free page left alone, and the sprite file itself still served byte for byte as it is on disk.
    The human deleted the C2PA manifest from sprite.svg afterwards; the guard stays so that if provenance ever
    comes back to the file it does not ride into every page.


43.  Context fill reads 0% while the pill says Waiting. [py] DONE 2026-10-24
    Same root as item 35, one layer further down. live.prompt_tokens / generated / max_tokens / elapsed_s were
    each gated on status["busy"], and busy is False between engine rounds - so during a tool wait the Monitor's
    gauge computed used = 0 while status still held prompt_tokens=2297, generated=5, max_tokens=5887. Measured
    before and after with a probe against the fake MCP server: all four fields None, then populated.
    Fix: `shown = busy or tool_running` gates those fields - the tool wait is part of the turn, so its numbers
    belong to the turn's status. tok_s stays gated on state == "generating": there is no token rate during a
    wait, and inventing one would be worse than showing none.
    Test: serve/test_mcp.py catches a live running-tool sample and asserts prompt_tokens and elapsed_s are not
    None. Control: gating on busy alone reproduces it - "unexpectedly None".


44.  Hovering the Monitor tab prefetches its answer, so the cards paint at once. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Monitor is the one tab that gets nothing while you are elsewhere - Chat and About already poll their own
    tiers, so their data is at most one interval old when you land on them. So only Monitor is prefetched, on
    mouseenter; the human's own note ("prefetch is only necessary for Monitor, as it's already polling for
    lite") is what narrowed my first per-tab version down to this.
    Three guards, because a mouse crossing a tab bar is not a request:
      - 250 ms of intent (HOVER_INTENT_MS): a sweep past never fires, and leaving inside the window cancels the
        armed timer, so changing your mind costs nothing
      - a 10 s freshness window (FULL_FRESH_MS) against lastFullAt, which the ordinary poll chain also sets -
        so while the Monitor is open its 1 s poll makes hovering free
      - one at a time: `prefetching` is held for the life of the request, so repeated hovers do not stack
    It fetches outside the poll scheduler (schedulePoll owns that chain) so it cannot fork a second one - the
    bug item 40 had to fix. A failed prefetch is swallowed: it is a warm-up, not a source of truth, and the
    poll chain retries on its own cadence.
    Tests: serve/test_chat_sidebar.py case 44 drives hoverTab/leaveTab against the recorded setTimeout queue -
    no fetch on hover alone, none if you leave inside the window, exactly one when it fires, none on a second
    hover while fresh, one more once stale, none while already on the Monitor, and none for About. Four
    controls (no intent window, no freshness window, leave does not cancel, prefetch fires anyway) each fail.
    The stub grew a recording setTimeout/clearTimeout and a fetch log for this; its addEventListener stays
    one-handler-per-type, because 20 existing call sites invoke it directly.


45.  Expand the thinking and the running tool call, then collapse them when they are done; and stop the drawer
    asking the same question four times. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Asked out of band, in two steps: first "expand the current thinking and any running tool call, then
    collapse them when they are done", then (from the human, seeing it work) "too many options in the menu".
    One change, because the first one shipped as a fourth switch beside three that already overlapped with it.
    THE CONTROL. "Show thinking" (s-show) is now a three-way segmented control, the same shape as Thinking:
    "Folded" / "Fold when done" / "Always", default "Fold when done". It decides both kinds of block, thinking
    and tool calls, from one key (settings.show, DEFAULTS app.js:646). The drawer went from 9 switches + 1
    segmented control to 6 switches + 2 (counted in index.html both ways), because these four are gone:
    s-show (a switch), s-openthink, s-opentools, s-autofold.
      - toolOpen (app.js:1026): folded -> never; live -> open only while the call is live, toolLive being
        state writing or running (app.js:1025), so it folds in the frame the result lands; always -> open.
      - syncThink (app.js:1225): the same three answers for a thinking block, through liveRun (app.js:1224).
      - MIGRATION (migrateShow, app.js:653, run on load): a browser with the old booleans stored maps them
        once - autofold true -> live, either keep-open switch -> always, show false -> folded, the old
        default -> live (it had no name: thinking open while it streams, calls closed). fold beats keep-open
        when both were on, the precedence that already applied. Harness case 49 covers all six, plus that the
        drawer shows the mapped answer, that Apply writes the word and no old key, and that a new-style object
        passes straight through.
    WHEN IS A THOUGHT OVER. "The last run while streaming" is not it: while a tool round runs thinkRuns still
    reports one run (the next round's reasoning has not arrived), so that rule holds a thought open through the
    whole call. Two signals, both derived: cut (runs[i].cut, app.js:1143 - a call starts where the run ends,
    refreshed per paint beside secs at app.js:1288) and tstart (how much answer text existed when the run
    began, stamped in send() as m.rts[index] = m.text.length, app.js:1581 - text grown past it means the model
    started writing). m.rts is numbers only, never text, so the replay tripwire (harness case 33) is byte for
    byte unchanged - asserted. tstart is NOT refreshed per paint: it is fixed when the run begins, and a
    control that tried to prove the refresh failed to fail, which is how the dead line was found and deleted.
    A chat with no stamp reports null and is never folded by the text rule.
    WHO OPENED IT. toolSync sets d.open itself (app.js:1093) and the #chat toggle listener records any toggle as
    the reader's choice (t.open = d.open), so an auto-open pinned a block open and the collapse never came. The
    thinking block had a guard for this (det._auto, set app.js:1225, consumed app.js:1421); the tool block had
    none. A flag was added, and THAT was still wrong: a browser queues the toggle event, so two paints leave two
    events in flight, the first eats the flag and the second is read as a click - which is the reported symptom,
    "calls fold only at the very end", because send() deletes t.open there (app.js:1613) and toolOpen
    short-circuits on it (app.js:1026). The tool branch now compares instead (if (d.open === toolOpen(t)) return;,
    app.js:1415): idempotent, so any number of queued events is the same. The thinking branch keeps its flag and
    has the same weakness - if a thinking block is ever reported stuck open, give it the same treatment.
    Tests: harness 45 (both kinds, one paint at a time: open live, folded settled, all three options on the
    same message), 46 and 47 (driven through send() with a real fake stream, so the m.rts stamp is covered -
    46 run 0, 47 the after-tool run at index 1), 49 (the migration and the drawer). serve/test_chat_sidebar.py
    test_show_thinking_is_one_three_way_control_for_both_kinds and
    test_the_fold_knows_when_a_thought_is_over pin the wiring. ok - lines 42 -> 47, def test_ 31 -> 32.
    NOT measured: what folding costs or saves in a browser - no browser on this box. The visible proof is the
    human's eyes.


46.  A click on a tool block held forever, and was written into the chat record. [ui] DONE 2026-10-24
    Found while doing 45, and the reason the human saw "the thinking closes but the calls stay open": with
    fold on and keep-open off, a fresh stream folded correctly (measured: state=done -> open=false), but ONE
    tap on a block set t.open, and toolOpen short-circuits whenever t.open is set - so no control could ever
    reclaim it. cleanMessages spreads ...m, so t.open went into the IndexedDB record and came back on every
    reload. The thinking block never had this bug: its "the reader touched this" is dataset.touched on the DOM
    element, which renderChat throws away. That asymmetry is why one kind folded and the other did not.
    Decided (the human's call, option 1 of three): a click is display state and holds for the turn, no longer.
      - send() drops it when the turn ends (app.js:1610), before the final paint re-decides from settings;
      - cleanMessages strips it on write (app.js:693);
      - openHistory strips it on READ (dropToolOpen, app.js:692, applied at 771 and 773) - records an older
        page already wrote still carry open: true, and without this they would pin the block forever anyway.
    m.md is kept: the human chose that per message and asked for it to survive a reload.
    Tests: harness 48 (a click during a turn is dropped by send(); a stored open:true from an older page is
    ignored; a click on a FINISHED message's block is recorded in memory and not written out). Both strips
    need their own assertion - the read strip masks the write strip, and a control that removed only
    cleanMessages' strip passed until case 48's last three lines were added.
    Also fixed on the way, because it was hiding this: the harness's IndexedDB stub aliased reads and writes
    (getAll returned the live record objects, put stored the object it was given), so case 5's rename
    "reached the record" through shared identity while actually saving a different chat. It now structured-
    clones on both sides like real IDB, and case 5 saves the chat it renamed. LESSONS.md has the entry.
